    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d397">

                <pb facs="0442" n="430" sameAs="frus:frus1943v05/pg430"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Pacific War/3327</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-07-09T18:00:00-03:00">July 9, 1943—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:24 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1526. Minister for Foreign Affairs informed my Chilean colleague<anchor n="27"
                        xml:id="d397-f1"/> today that Japanese Ambassador<anchor n="28"
                        xml:id="d397-f2"/> called on him yesterday and was even more menacing than
                    at the time he called to protest against Radio Communications decree. He
                    cautioned the Minister against his Government breaking relations with Japan,
                    saying that they would defeat the United States and would then retaliate against
                    those countries which had taken an unfriendly position. The Minister told him
                    that if Japan proceeded against any American country, Argentina would declare
                    war.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
